<h2>Hypothesis</h2>
<p>
  This is not a claim about nature but a claim about the instrument: the engine, run in
  the shape the published BFF work used, reproduces the emergence of self-replicators.
  A well-mixed soup — every interaction pairs a cell with a partner drawn uniformly from
  the whole world — of 2<sup>17</sup> tapes should leave the random-soup regime within
  the epoch budget: <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> collapses,
  <span class="mono">replicator_count</span> rises off zero and stays there.
</p>
<p>
  The falsifier is blunt. If this control never transitions,
  suspect the interpreter or the pairing rule, not the hypothesis:
  the sweeps of DESIGN §1.3 are only worth reading once the engine has been shown to
  produce the effect they measure.
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  A 512×256 torus — 131 072 cells, 2<sup>17</sup> tapes — of 64-byte tapes
  (<span class="mono">tape_len = 64</span>), <span class="mono">radius = 0</span> for the
  well-mixed neighbourhood, <span class="mono">max_steps = 8192</span> per interaction,
  50 000 epochs per run. Two arms, <span class="mono">mutation_rate = 0</span> and the
  engine default <span class="mono">2<sup>-12</sup> = 2.44 × 10<sup>-4</sup></span>, with
  seeds 1, 2 and 3 each: six runs. Sampling is sparse for a world this size —
  <span class="mono">sample_every = 50</span> and
  <span class="mono">snapshot_every = 2000</span>, 25 full-world snapshots per run instead
  of the 500 the engine's default cadence would write. The runs carry queue priority 10,
  ahead of every sweep, because nothing else in the lab means anything until they land.
</p>

<h2>Result</h2>
<p>
  Partial, and not yet the reading the hypothesis asked for. It is a reading and not a
  claim for three reasons: the runs are far from their epoch horizon, the mutated arm has
  moved in two of its three seeds and no more, and the census that would make the move mean
  something is not yet resolved here. What follows states shapes; the per-run numbers behind
  them drift with every sample and are in the evidence table below, which reads the runs
  live.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>The instrument produces the signature.</strong> The mutated arm has transitioned
  in one of its three seeds, and it transitioned the way the mutation-rate sweep's flagged
  runs do: <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> collapses inside a few sampling intervals,
  <span class="mono">distinct_tapes</span> falls from one-per-cell and keeps falling,
  <span class="mono">copy_rate</span> lifts off exactly zero,
  <span class="mono">op_density</span> rises and <span class="mono">entropy_bits</span>
  drops — one joint move, not five independent ones. Two of the three
  <span class="mono">mutation_rate = 0</span> controls sit at the random-soup baseline, the
  third is the negative result discussed below, and the last mutated seed has not moved. So
  the interpreter and the pairing rule
  do produce the published signature in the published shape; the harness is not what the
  sweeps were measuring.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>And the census is not zero.</strong> A second mutated seed carries the largest
  <span class="mono">replicator_count</span> in the lab — thousands of replicating cells at
  its peak, at a <span class="mono">copy_rate</span> two orders of magnitude above the soup
  floor — which is the shape the mutation-rate sweep shows: a census peak near the
  transition rather than a level that holds. What is missing is a stored world that brackets
  the peak. This control snapshots every 2 000 epochs, and no snapshot falls inside the
  window where the count was high, so the peak rests on in-run samples alone and cannot be
  rescored the way the mutation-rate peaks were — where a stored world does bracket a peak,
  a rescore confirms the count and finds a tape passing the trial. Truncation of the ranking
  is ruled out either way: widening the cut to the top 16, 64 and 256 tapes returns
  identical metrics at all three widths. So the census here is not yet resolved rather than
  not evidence, and the falsifier above is neither triggered nor retired.
</p>

<h2>What the zero-mutation control shows</h2>
<p>
  The sharpest result of this experiment is in its negative arm. One
  <span class="mono">mutation_rate = 0</span> seed collapses
  <span class="mono">entropy_bits</span> outright and drives
  <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> to a tenth of the soup floor with a census of
  zero throughout. With no mutation there is nothing novel to find, so nothing there can be
  a replicator that emerged; what the collapse is, is a copying cascade of the tapes the
  seed handed the world — the control behaving as a control. That is direct evidence that an
  entropy collapse on its own is not evidence of a replicator, and it is why the census, not
  the compression, is what decides.
</p>
<p>
  The measurement this experiment still owes is a stored world inside a census peak: a finer
  <span class="mono">snapshot_every</span> for a world this size, and the snapshot forced on
  the sample where a transition settles, so that every peak the samples report can be
  rescored against the world that produced it. Until one of this control's peaks is rescored
  that way, the emergence it shows is read from in-run observables only.
</p>

<p>
  The gap in these runs is harness history, not signal. All six failed between epoch 546
  and epoch 633 against a read limit in the runner's lab-mode client — its 10 MiB cap on an
  API response, too small for a world this size — fixed by PR #17, which raised the limit
  to 256 MiB, and they were requeued from their last snapshot. A container recreate
  released them a second time, and they resumed from their snapshots again.
</p>
<p>
  The mutation-rate sweep's strongest transitions have since been confirmed in their stored
  worlds, so the census is real and the instrument can see a lineage. What is still open is
  this control's own census, and until it is resolved no sweep on this site is readable as a
  clean negative result. The mutation-rate finding is held on that basis.
</p>
